<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Panama Has Delicious Food! – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Planning Meals for My Family</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> Panama Has Delicious Food!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write short personal narratives about weekend activities. Interactive: Edit simple written work from peers for grammar and spelling collaboratively.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a short story about their weekend (e.g., "This weekend we have a special Panamanian meal with healthy food." "I want to cook."). Can check a peer's work and give feedback (e.g., "Not aet, e-a-t, eat."). Can share their weekend plans with the class (e.g., "This weekend, I am...").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e0e0;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's focus.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or pictures of various Panamanian dishes and ingredients (e.g., rice, beans, mangos, sancocho). Students quickly write down 3-4 food items they see or remember, emphasizing a brief writing activation. Teacher encourages students to use vocabulary like 'healthy', 'tasty', 'fruits', 'vegetables'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple sentence about a meal. For example, "This weekend, I can eat some delicious mangos." The teacher writes it on the board, pointing to 'can', 'eat', 'some', and 'mangos'. The teacher also briefly models the pronunciation of /v/ in "vegetables" and /f/ in "fruit" by saying the words clearly as they write them, reinforcing phonemic awareness from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the main verb in 'I can eat mangos'?" (Expected: eat)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we write 'mangos' or 'mangoes'?" (Expected: mangos)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'some' for many or a few?" (Expected: a few, or a quantity not specified)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario. Focuses on providing a clear model for the target writing skill.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario:</b> The teacher presents a short written text (a simple narrative) on the board or a projector about a child planning a weekend meal for their family. The text will incorporate the target grammar (Present simple, 'can', countable/uncountable nouns, quantifiers like 'some', 'many', sequencing adverbs like 'first', 'next') and vocabulary (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack, rice, beans, bread, water, juice, mangos, watermelon, tomatoes, lettuce, fruits, vegetables, meal, cook, boil, bake, fry, steam, cut, slice, eat, enjoy, tasty, salty, sour, sweet, healthy, fresh).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt (3-4 sentences) titled "My Weekend Meal Plan."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example text:</p>
                <blockquote style="background-color: #f5f5f5; border-left: 3px solid #1a5276; margin: 0; padding: 10px; text-align: justify;">
                    "This weekend, my family wants to eat a special Panamanian meal for <b>lunch</b>. <b>First</b>, we <b>can cook</b> some <b>rice</b> and <b>beans</b>. My mom <b>can cut some healthy vegetables</b> like <b>tomatoes</b> and <b>lettuce</b> for a salad. We also have <b>many fresh fruits</b> like <b>mangos</b> and <b>watermelon</b> for a <b>snack</b>. We <b>enjoy</b> cooking together!"
                </blockquote>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner comprehends and expresses simple written information about familiar topics, such as food and family activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently. Then, the teacher asks basic gist questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What meal is the family planning?" (Expected: lunch)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What vegetables do they want to cut?" (Expected: tomatoes and lettuce)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What fruits do they have for a snack?" (Expected: mangos and watermelon)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students practice controlled and semi-controlled writing to reinforce target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using Present simple, modal 'can', countable/uncountable nouns with quantifiers ('some', 'many'), sequencing adverbs ('first', 'next'), and vocabulary related to food and meals (e.g., 'cook', 'eat', 'healthy', 'tasty', 'breakfast', 'lunch', 'dinner', 'fruits', 'vegetables').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences from the sample narrative or similar ones, but with blanks for target vocabulary, verbs, quantifiers, or sequencing adverbs. Students complete the sentences individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                            <li>This weekend, my family wants to eat a special Panamanian ____. (meal/snack)</li>
                            <li>_____, we can ____ some rice and beans. (First/Next, cook/eat)</li>
                            <li>My mom can cut ____ healthy vegetables. (some/many)</li>
                            <li>We ____ many fresh fruits. (have/want)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Sentence Construction (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher gives them sentence starters or prompts to write new sentences about planning meals. They must use specific vocabulary words and grammar points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example Prompts:
                        <ul style="margin-top: 5px;">
                            <li>Start with "This weekend, I can..." and write about a meal you want to cook. (Use 'cook' and 'some'.)</li>
                            <li>Write about your favorite healthy food. (Use 'fruits' or 'vegetables'.)</li>
                            <li>Describe what you eat for breakfast. (Use 'eat' and 'bread' or 'juice'.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Building & Peer Check:</b> Students remain in pairs. They receive a short, incomplete paragraph about a meal plan. They must collaboratively write 2-3 sentences to complete it, focusing on using sequencing adverbs and correct countable/uncountable nouns. Then, they exchange their completed paragraphs with another pair and check for simple grammar and spelling errors, reinforcing the interactive editing skill. The teacher circulates, providing support and correction, emphasizing suffixes like '-ing' in 'cooking' if it arises.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students produce original written work, focusing on fluency and completing the task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will individually write a short personal narrative (3-5 sentences) about their family's weekend meal plans, titled "My Family's Weekend Meal." They must include at least two sequencing adverbs ('first', 'next'), use 'can' to express ability or possibility, and incorporate at least three new vocabulary words related to food or preparation (e.g., 'cook', 'fry', 'steam', 'tasty', 'healthy', 'some rice', 'many fruits'). The objective is to write a coherent, simple story describing their plans.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly relates to "Panama Has Delicious Food!" by asking students to think about meals they eat or would like to eat, and to "Planning Meals for My Family" as they describe their specific weekend meal preparations or choices. The teacher encourages students to include Panamanian food if possible.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus on fluency, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion:</b> While the primary focus is individual writing, the teacher reminds students of the peer-checking skills practiced earlier. Students are encouraged to focus on conveying their meal plans clearly. The teacher will check for the completion of the narrative and the inclusion of target linguistic features.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment and self-reflection to gauge understanding and progress.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Peer assessment using a simple checklist and teacher observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their "My Family's Weekend Meal" narratives with a peer. Each student uses a simple checklist to review their partner's writing. The checklist includes:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner write 3-5 sentences? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner use 'first' or 'next'? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did my partner use 'can'? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are there new food words? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can I understand the story? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">*Optional*: Did they try to use countable/uncountable nouns correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> (e.g., food items, verbs, adjectives from the list).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and coherence:</b> (Is the narrative easy to understand?).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> (Appropriate use of Present simple, 'can', basic countable/uncountable nouns).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> (During peer checking, were students able to give simple, constructive feedback?).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small piece of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new food word you used in your story?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you write a plan for a meal you want to cook?" (Yes/No/A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing your meal plan was easy? What was difficult?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing your partner helped you correct in your writing?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., Students effectively used sequencing adverbs; peer assessment led to meaningful corrections).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson or future writing tasks? (e.g., More direct instruction on verb conjugation for present simple, clearer distinction between 'some' and 'many' in writing, or more practice with specific vocabulary).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on the written narratives. Students will use their meal plans to present and summarize information, possibly explaining their choices to the class or creating a visual menu. This will integrate the written product into a broader communicative context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>